<!--
    A request built on the frame by a zeroing loop and then field stores must
    print each field store after its loop.

    The code is x86-64 at its link address (-nostdlib -static
    -Ttext=0x401000, -fcf-protection=none), gcc 11 -O2
    -fstack-protector-strong:

        struct hdr { unsigned len; unsigned short type, flags;
                     unsigned seq, pid; };
        struct req { struct hdr n; struct ghdr g; char buf[4096]; };
        extern int fam;
        int nl(int argc, char **argv) {
          struct req req = { .n = { .type = fam, .flags = 1, .len = 0x14 },
                             .g = { .cmd = 3, .ver = 1 } };
          if (argc > 0) { ... parse(argc, argv, &req.n, 3) ...
                          talk(rth, &req.n, &ans) ... }
          struct req req2 = { .n = { .type = fam, .flags = 0x301,
                              .len = 0x14 }, .g = { .cmd = 3, .ver = 1 } };
          if (sendit(rth, &req2, req2.n.len) < 0) return -1;
          return show(rth, 0); }

    Each request is zeroed with `rep stos` and then gets `fam` stored into its
    type.  The register holding `fam` must not be merged into req2 across
    req2's zeroing loop: the call-crossing test refuses that merge when the
    register's live range holds a STORE that may write req2, and its walk
    (`Cover::intersect_op_set`) skipped the set's block after every block it
    shared with the range, so it never saw the loop's STORE.  The C then
    printed req2's type before the loop that clears it, and req's type was
    copied out of req2.  With stackstoreguard's indexed-store guards this is
    the shape of iproute2 `ip` -O2 0x64ca0.  Pass 2 (stackstoreguard off)
    prints what main prints.
-->
